  policy_proposed_eff_dt:
    type: date
    semantic_type: iso_date
    widget:
      page: 1
      rect:
      - 22.0
      - 396.0
      - 130.0
      - 408.0
      widget_type: text
      path: F[0].P1[0].Policy_EffectiveDate_A[0]
    x_petey_retype:
    - pattern_dropped
    - description_from_tooltip
    description: The effective date of the policy. The date that the terms and conditions
      of the policy commence. As used here, the date on which the terms and conditions
      of the policy will commence. For assigned risk business being submitted with
      the ACORD 133 use the effective date on that form, following state mandated
      rules.
    x_petey_description_source: tooltip
  policy_proposed_exp_dt:
    type: date
    semantic_type: iso_date
    widget:
      page: 1
      rect:
      - 137.0
      - 396.0
      - 245.0
      - 408.0
      widget_type: text
      path: F[0].P1[0].Policy_ExpirationDate_A[0]
    x_petey_retype:
    - pattern_dropped
    - description_from_tooltip
    description: The date on which the terms and conditions of the policy will expire.
      As used here, the date on which the terms and conditions of the policy will
      expire. The normal policy period (effective date to expiration date) is one
      year. However, a policy may be issued for any length of time up to a maximum
      of three years. Certain rules and endorsements must be used if the policy is
      written for more than one year. It may be necessary to use Effective and Expiration
      Dates that do not indicate a one year term, to concur with other policies.
    x_petey_description_source: tooltip
  normal_anniversary_rating_dt:
    type: date
    semantic_type: iso_date
    widget:
      page: 1
      rect:
      - 252.0
      - 396.0
      - 371.0
      - 408.0
      widget_type: text
      path: F[0].P1[0].Policy_NormalAnniversaryRatingDate_A[0]
    x_petey_retype:
    - pattern_dropped
    - description_from_tooltip
    description: The rates used are normally in effect on the effective date of the
      policy. NCCI Manual rules require that the rates apply for a period of one year.
      If a policy is cancelled or short-termed, the rating bureau requires the original
      effective date to be considered the Normal Anniversary Rating Date for both
      rates and experience modifications. This is temporary and will last until the
      next renewal when the new policy effective date will again determine the rates.
      The rule is intended to prevent wholesale cancellations by insureds and companies
      to take advantage of rate and/or rule changes. For cancelled or short-termed
      polices, enter the original effective date.
    x_petey_description_source: tooltip
